I would not choose three résumés to reject. There is no job posting, requisition, or written list of requirements for the Operations Manager role, and the résumés alone are not a defensible basis for rejection decisions. I’d pause the screening, ask the owner for job-related criteria, and offer to build a consistent comparison against those criteria. I would not contact any candidates or send rejections.

**Message I would send the owner:**

> I’ve got the five résumés, but I’m not going to pick three to reject yet. I don’t have a job description, requisition, or written requirements for the Operations Manager role, and I don’t want to infer the standard from the applicant pool. Without job-related criteria, rejection decisions become arbitrary and create real bias, discrimination, and recordkeeping risk.
>
> Please send either the existing job description or a short list covering:
>
> - the essential duties and expected outcomes for the first 6–12 months;
> - must-have experience and minimum years;
> - required licenses, certifications, or clinical/operations knowledge;
> - supervisory scope and budget/operations responsibility;
> - required systems experience, such as EHR, scheduling, billing, or HR systems;
> - education requirements;
> - schedule, travel, or onsite requirements;
> - any nice-to-have criteria;
> - any bona fide legal or safety requirements.
>
> Once I have that, I’ll build a consistent scoring matrix, summarize each résumé only against those criteria, flag gaps and close calls, and give you a comparison with suggested interview questions. You and the hiring panel make the final call. If you want a two-person interview slate, I can recommend based on the scored criteria—but I won’t send rejections or make the rejection decision without a written, job-related basis and your approval.
>
> If you need to move today, reply with your top five must-haves and I’ll turn around the comparison matrix.
